<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Light Under The Door
</h4>

<p>
"The Craftworks initiative." Anybody out there know what that is?
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:mkruse@sptimes.com">Kruse</a>
	

 at August 06,2007 23:39
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2197"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Light Under The Door
</h4>

<p>
Hi fellow Gangrey folks (Gangreyees? Gangroids?),<br />
<br />
This is Kevin Pang at the ChiTrib...long time listener, first time caller, as they say. I'm part of this CraftWork cabal at the Tribune. Not only is it a nod to our favorite 70's German electronica band, but an initiative started earlier this year to promote smart writing and storytelling in the daily paper. <br />
<br />
We've asked ourselves: how could we take those obligatory day-after-Thanksgiving-shopping, parade, first day of spring et al stories and make them sing? Can a business story be told as a tick-tock? How about making the daily updates of a federal mob trial read like a summer novel? <br />
<br />
So a bunch of us got together and began organizing monthly brownbag lunches on writing and reporting techniques. Bless the company for providing cookies and soda. Then, we convinced the editors to bring in the great Jacqui Banaszynski to conduct a two-day mandatory workshop on story organization, writing ledes and endings etc. And just yesterday, the news of having Louise, one of the best writers and reporters in the country, agree to take on this role, well, that's as good as it gets.<br />
<br />
I was telling Ben that the mood in newsrooms is pretty crappy now, with brass slashing and burning positions. For our bosses to create this new position is putting faith in this mission that the readers of this blog -- you guys -- so care about. So yeah, I'm psyched for my newspaper, but I'm also psyched for journalism. Corny? Perhaps, but I get the sense I'm just preachin' to the choir.<br />
<br />
Kevin<br />
<br />

</p>
